== Diagnostic Takeaways on Demographics:
1. *Population Size & Growth:* India and China each house approximately 1.4 billion people, collectively accounting for more than a third of humanity. However, China's annual population growth rate dropped to *0.46%* due to the strict enforcement of the One-Child Policy (1979), whereas Pakistan exhibits a rapid *1.98%* growth rate.
2. *Population Density Contrast:* China spans an immense geographic territory (over 9.6 million sq km), giving it a low population density of *150 persons per sq km*. In contrast, India accommodates a comparable population within a land area one-third the size of China, creating severe land pressure with *435 persons per sq km*.
3. *Sex Ratio Distortions:* In all three nations, the sex ratio is deeply unfavorable to women (below 950 females per 1,000 males), driven by patriarchal son preference, female feticide, and higher maternal mortality. China displays the most acute skew (*926*), directly exacerbated by the One-Child Policy, which led to widespread female feticide.
4. *Urbanisation Trajectory:* China is predominantly urbanized (*65%*), fueled by massive rural-to-urban labor migration into coastal industrial manufacturing centers. India (*35%*) and Pakistan (*37%*) remain predominantly rural nations.

