== 2. Contrasting Paths of Structural Transformation:
- *China (Classical Industrial Transformation):* China followed the textbook historical path of modern industrialization. After agricultural communes were reformed in 1978, surplus farm labor was systematically channeled into manufacturing industries located in Special Economic Zones. Industry accounts for *38% of Chinese GDP* (compared to 28% in India and 19% in Pakistan), making China the unquestioned *"Factory of the World"*.
- *India (Service-Led Leapfrogging):* India bypassed the traditional industrial manufacturing stage. While agriculture's contribution to GDP shrank from over 50% in 1950 to 16% today, the industrial manufacturing sector failed to expand proportionally. Instead, India leapfrogged directly into the *Services Sector* (IT, software development, telecommunications, financial services, BPO), which contributes *56% of Indian GDP*.
- *The Indian Agricultural Distortion:* Despite contributing only 16% to national GDP, agriculture in India continues to absorb *over 43% of the total national workforce*. This vast discrepancy is the primary driver of pervasive *disguised unemployment* and depressed rural per capita incomes in India.
- *Pakistan (Premature Stagnation):* Pakistan exhibits high dependence on agriculture for both GDP (24%) and jobs (37%), alongside an anemic manufacturing sector (19% of GDP). Growth has been sustained predominantly by informal commerce, tertiary services, and overseas remittances.

