= Human Development Indicators (HDI) & Comparative Scorecard

Economic growth is merely an instrument; the ultimate objective of development is the enrichment of human life. The United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) measures human development across three core dimensions: (1) A long and healthy life, (2) Knowledge, and (3) A decent standard of living.

#table(
  columns: (1.3fr, 1.1fr, 1.1fr, 1.1fr),
  table.header[*Human Development Metric*][*India*][*China*][*Pakistan*],
  [*HDI Rank* (out of ~191 nations)], [~134 (Medium HD)], [*~79* (High HD)], [~161 (Low HD)],
  [*Life Expectancy at Birth (Years)*], [67.2 to 70.0], [*78.2*], [66.1],
  [*Mean Years of Schooling*], [6.7 years], [*8.1 years*], [4.5 years],
  [*Gross National Income (GNI) per capita (PPP USD)*], [~USD 6,590], [*~USD 17,504*], [~USD 4,624],
  [*Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR per 1 lakh births)*], [103], [*10 to 18*], [154 to 186],
  [*Infant Mortality Rate (IMR per 1,000 live births)*], [27 to 28], [*6 to 7*], [53 to 56],
  [*Extreme Poverty Headcount (< USD 2.15/day)*], [Substantially declined], [*Virtually Eliminated*], [Persistent / High],
  [*Access to Improved Sanitation (%)*], [~71%], [*~92%*], [~60%],
)

== Diagnostic Takeaways on Human Development:
1. *China's Decisive Leadership:* On every standard composite human development indicator, China outperforms both India and Pakistan by a wide margin:
   - Chinese citizens live an average of *78.2 years* (nearly 8 to 11 years longer than Indians or Pakistanis).
   - Infant Mortality Rate in China (*6 to 7 per 1,000*) is comparable to Western European nations, whereas India (*28*) and Pakistan (*53*) suffer from high infant deaths.
   - Maternal Mortality in Pakistan (*154*) and India (*103*) reflects severe deficits in rural emergency obstetric care compared to China (*10–18*).
   - China's per capita income in Purchasing Power Parity (*~USD 17,504*) is nearly *2.7 times that of India* and *3.8 times that of Pakistan*.
2. *India's Middle Standing:* India sits solidly in the *Medium Human Development* tier (ranked ~134th). While India has made immense progress in life expectancy, literacy, and extreme poverty reduction since 1991, it continues to trail behind China in public health access, child undernutrition, and mean years of schooling.
3. *Pakistan's Lagging Performance:* Pakistan is categorized in the *Low Human Development* tier (ranked ~161st), hobbled by low schooling (mean of 4.5 years), high maternal mortality, and persistent rural poverty.

