= Practice Questions — Part 2 (Q6 to Q10)

#mcq(6, [Which country among the three neighbours initiated the strict "One-Child Policy" in 1979 to control population growth, and what was its major demographic consequence?], (
  [India; it caused the population growth rate to jump to 3% per annum],
  [Pakistan; it caused massive rural-to-urban migration],
  [China; it drastically arrested population growth but caused a severe skew in the sex ratio and an aging workforce],
  [China; it caused an immediate doubling of the fertility rate],
))
#solution(6, [China enacted the One-Child Policy in 1979. While it successfully reduced the annual population growth rate to under 0.5%, it induced a severe deficit of female births (distorting the sex ratio to 926) and accelerated the aging of its demographic pyramid.], answer: "C")

#mcq(7, [In terms of structural sectoral transformation, why is China celebrated as the "Factory of the World", whereas India is characterized as a "Service Leapfrogger"?], (
  [Because China produces only raw materials, while India manufactures all global hardware],
  [Because China's growth was driven by secondary industrial manufacturing (38% of GDP), whereas India leapfrogged from agriculture directly into tertiary services (56% of GDP)],
  [Because India has zero employment in the services sector],
  [Because China banned all service sector activities in 1978],
))
#solution(7, [China followed the classic structural transformation path, building an unparalleled industrial manufacturing export base in SEZs (accounting for 38% of GDP). India bypassed the manufacturing expansion phase, leaping directly from an agrarian economy into a service-dominated economy (accounting for 56% of GDP).], answer: "B")

#mcq(8, [Despite contributing only around 16% to national GDP, approximately what proportion of India's total workforce remains employed in agriculture, signifying pervasive disguised unemployment?], (
  [Approximately 10%],
  [Approximately 25%],
  [Over 43%],
  [100%],
))
#solution(8, [A major structural imbalance in the Indian economy is that while agriculture contributes only ~16% to GDP, it continues to engage over 43% of the total labor force, generating acute disguised unemployment and low agricultural labor productivity.], answer: "C")

#mcq(9, [According to the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) Human Development Index (HDI) rankings, what is the correct relative rank ordering of the three neighbours from highest to lowest human development?], (
  [India $==>$ Pakistan $==>$ China],
  [China $==>$ Pakistan $==>$ India],
  [Pakistan $==>$ India $==>$ China],
  [China (High) $==>$ India (Medium) $==>$ Pakistan (Low)],
))
#solution(9, [On all standard composite HDI metrics (life expectancy, schooling, per capita GNI, infant/maternal mortality), China ranks highest (~79th, High Human Development), India ranks in the middle (~134th, Medium Human Development), and Pakistan ranks lowest (~161st, Low Human Development).], answer: "D")

#mcq(10, [What is the fundamental limitation of standard Human Development Index (HDI) metrics when evaluating a nation's total development, and what concept addresses this gap?], (
  [HDI measures only industrial output; addressed by Green GDP],
  [HDI measures only material capabilities (income, health, literacy) and completely ignores civic, political, and democratic rights; addressed by "Liberty Indicators"],
  [HDI counts only male citizens; addressed by the Gender Inequality Index],
  [HDI ignores life expectancy; addressed by per capita income],
))
#solution(10, [Standard HDI focuses strictly on quantitative material capabilities (longevity, educational attainment, and income), completely omitting political freedoms, democratic rights, and rule of law. Economists propose "Liberty Indicators" (measuring free speech, democratic elections, independent judiciary) to capture these vital democratic dimensions.], answer: "B")

