= Practice Questions — Part 3 (Q11 to Q15)

#mcq(11, [During China's "Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution" (1966–1976), what mandate was enforced upon students and professionals by Mao Zedong's Red Guards?], (
  [They were sent to the United States to study modern finance],
  [They were ordered to build nuclear power plants in urban centers],
  [They were required to launch private e-commerce corporations],
  [They were sent to the rural countryside to work and learn from manual agricultural peasants],
))
#solution(11, [During the Cultural Revolution (1966–1976), Mao mobilized student-led Red Guards to conduct an ideological purge. Millions of urban university students, intellectuals, and professionals were forcibly sent to the countryside to perform manual field labour and learn from peasants.], answer: "D")

#mcq(12, [Which primary factor explains why China was able to achieve rapid industrial manufacturing success post-1978, compared to India and Pakistan?], (
  [China had higher foreign debt liabilities than India and Pakistan],
  [China had established strong foundational social infrastructure (universal primary education, rural health, land equity) BEFORE launching market reforms in 1978],
  [China completely banned all foreign multinational corporations from entering its economy],
  [China possessed zero agricultural farmland],
))
#solution(12, [Before 1978, China's command system built universal primary schooling, basic health networks, and land equity. When Deng Xiaoping opened market reforms and SEZs, China possessed a healthy, literate, disciplined rural labor force that was smoothly absorbed into manufacturing factories.], answer: "B")

#mcq(13, [Why did Pakistan's economic growth decelerate significantly after initial growth spurts in the 1960s and 1980s?], (
  [Because Pakistan abolished all private enterprise permanently],
  [Because of political instability, recurrent military takeovers, low investment in human capital, and excessive dependence on foreign aid and volatile remittances],
  [Because of over-investment in heavy industrial capital goods manufacturing],
  [Because the United Nations placed a total embargo on Pakistani cotton exports],
))
#solution(13, [Pakistan's economic fragility stems from governance instability, frequent military regimes, low public capital investment in education and health, and over-reliance on external financing (US strategic aid, IMF loans, and Gulf remittances) rather than developing a resilient domestic industrial base.], answer: "B")

#mcq(14, [Which of the three neighbours boasts the highest proportion of its total population residing in urban areas (approx. 65%), driven by massive coastal industrialization?], (
  [India],
  [Pakistan],
  [China],
  [All three countries have identical 35% urbanisation rates],
))
#solution(14, [China has achieved an urbanisation rate of approximately 65%, driven by massive rural-to-urban labor migration into coastal industrial manufacturing zones. By comparison, India (~35%) and Pakistan (~37%) remain predominantly rural nations.], answer: "C")

#mcq(15, [In which country was the "Commune System" introduced in agriculture, where peasant households collectively farmed state-owned land, and in which year was it launched?], (
  [Pakistan in 1947],
  [India in 1951],
  [China in 1958 (under the Great Leap Forward)],
  [China in 1991 (under the New Economic Policy)],
))
#solution(15, [The Commune System was initiated in rural China in 1958 as part of the Great Leap Forward campaign, grouping millions of peasant households into 26,000 collective agricultural production units to cultivate state-owned land.], answer: "C")
